Since last week freehire speaks [OJCP](https://spec.ojcp.dev/) — an open draft
standard for how an AI agent asks a job board for jobs. An agent reads one file
at `/.well-known/ojcp.json`, learns which tools we offer, and can search the
catalogue, pull a posting's full detail, and ask about an employer without anyone
writing an integration for us specifically. That's the point of a standard: the
agent doesn't have to know our name.

Implementing one is also the fastest way to find out what it can't say. A
standard is a shape, and every shape leaves something out. We hit four such
places, and rather than shrug at them we measured how much each one costs, and
sent the figures to the people writing the spec.

## Measuring instead of asserting

"The standard is missing X" is an opinion. "The standard is missing X, and here
is how many real postings it affects" is something a spec editor can act on.

So each gap got a number, from a 0.5% random sample of our 5.9 million open
public postings — about 30,000 postings, drawn on 17 September. Not a survey of
the industry. One catalogue being specific about what it loses.

## A job can be open in more than one country

**2.7% of our open postings name more than one country.** At our size that's
roughly 160,000 jobs.

The standard's location field holds one place. That sounds harmless until you
try to fill it. Say a role is open in Germany, Poland and Spain. Write Germany
and you've asserted a restriction the employer never made — an agent filtering
for Spain now throws the job away, and the candidate never sees it. So we do the
only honest thing available and leave the field empty, which makes the posting
look location-less, which is *also* worse for the agent than the truth.

Two wrong answers and no right one. The fix turned out to be small: the
vocabulary the standard builds on already allows a list here, so the standard was
stricter than the thing it extends. We proposed accepting either one place or a
list, so nothing built against the old shape breaks.

## Three seniority levels the standard has no word for

The standard names six levels: entry, mid, senior, lead, director, executive.
Here's how the postings that state a level actually distribute:

| Level | Share | Has a standard word? |
|---|---|---|
| senior | 42.9% | yes |
| lead | 17.0% | yes |
| junior | 11.2% | folds into *entry* |
| **intern** | **10.4%** | **no** |
| c-level | 7.6% | maps to *executive* |
| **staff** | **3.9%** | **no** |
| **principal** | **3.8%** | **no** |
| middle | 3.1% | maps to *mid* |

**18.1% sit on a level the standard cannot name.** The rows that fold in do so
cleanly — c-level really is executive. These three don't.

An internship is not a junior job. Someone looking for one does not want the
other, and collapsing them returns the wrong postings in both directions. Staff
and principal are the senior individual-contributor track: an engineer who
doesn't want to manage people and has spent a decade getting good instead.
Flatten them into "senior" and an agent searching for a principal role quietly
receives ordinary senior ones.

We proposed adding all three. It's a small change — the field was already open
text, so the values were legal on the wire; what was missing was the *documented*
list, which is the only thing an agent can safely be written against.

## The two we didn't rush

The other two gaps are real but harder, and the spec's editor pushed back in ways
that made both proposals better.

The first is eligibility. We hold whether a job sponsors a visa, whether it pays
to relocate you, and whether it requires a security clearance. None of these fit
in the standard, and all three are hard gates — reading them is the difference
between applying and wasting an application. The agreed direction is that each
gate must have **three** states, not two: offered, not offered, and *not stated*.
If "we don't know" collapses into "no", the standard starts asserting
restrictions employers never wrote — exactly the same mistake as picking one
country out of three.

The second is the hardest one. We compute a hedged signal for whether a posting
still looks like a live opening — how long it's been up, whether it's vanished
from the employer's own careers site, whether applicants report silence. Today
that ships as prose, which an agent can't filter on. But publishing a structured
verdict about a *named employer* is a different kind of claim from publishing a
salary range, and providers each compute it differently, so the levels wouldn't
compare across catalogues anyway. The better idea, which we're now pursuing:
publish the *observations* and who made them, and let the agent form its own
judgement. That one's a full design discussion, not a quick patch.

## The same bug, in our own code

Here's the part that stung.

While writing up the seniority argument we went to check our own implementation —
and found we'd been making a version of the mistake we were complaining about.

We publish the levels the standard has no word for under our own names. That was
deliberate and it's right: an unfamiliar word is visibly unknown to an agent,
while a wrong one isn't. But the *other* direction — an agent sending a level
back to us as a search filter — only understood the standard's five. So an agent
could read `intern` off one of our postings, send `intern` straight back as a
filter, and be told we didn't understand it. And when we don't understand a
filter, the answer widens. It asked for internships and got the entire catalogue.

Four of our eight levels behaved that way: intern, junior, staff and principal.
We were publishing a vocabulary we then refused to filter on.

It's fixed, and the fix came with a test that walks the real vocabulary out
through the projection and back in through the filter, and fails if a level
doesn't arrive as itself. The bug existed precisely because both halves were
written by hand in different files and nothing checked them against each other. A
test that listed the levels itself would have passed while the gap sat there —
the list has to come from the vocabulary, not from the test.

One behaviour changed along the way: asking for `entry` used to also return
internships, on the reasoning that someone wanting entry-level work wants both.
That was the right call only while `intern` was unsayable. Now it isn't, so entry
means junior roles, intern means internships, and for the first time you can ask
for junior roles *without* internships — which the old mapping made impossible.

## Why bother

None of this is visible on the site. No candidate will notice that a location
field accepts a list.

But the agents reading job listings today are reading them badly, and mostly by
scraping. A standard that can say "this role is open in three countries and
requires a clearance we'll tell you about upfront" is how that stops. The
standard only gets there if the people implementing it report what breaks,
with numbers attached, instead of quietly working around it.

Two of the four are written up and waiting on review. The other two are in
progress. We'll write about those when they land.
